      "explanation": "When a new epoch starts (block.timestamp >= _periodFinish), the contract correctly computes `totalRewardAmount = rewardAmount + clPool.rollover()` and syncs the pool with `rewardReserve = totalRewardAmount`, but it computes `rewardRate` using only `rewardAmount / epochTimeRemaining`. As a result, the emission schedule for the epoch is based only on the newly-notified rewards, while the rolled-over rewards are excluded from the rate. Those rollover tokens therefore are never emitted during the epoch. At the next epoch boundary they remain un-emitted and become `rollover()` again, and since the same flawed computation repeats every new epoch, rollover rewards can remain perpetually undistributed (effectively locked from stakers), causing long-term/ permanent loss of expected rewards for users and breaking the intended accounting between reserve and rate.",
      "attack_scenario": "1) Epoch N ends with remaining undistributed rewards in the pool accounting (clPool.rollover() > 0), e.g., due to low staked liquidity or mid-epoch reward changes.\n2) At the start of Epoch N+1, the (normal, non-compromised) DISTRIBUTION calls notifyRewardAmount(token, X).\n3) GaugeCL sets rewardReserve to X + rollover but sets rewardRate to X/epochTimeRemaining.\n4) During Epoch N+1, only ~X tokens worth of rewards are emitted; the rollover portion is not emitted and remains in reserve.\n5) At the end of Epoch N+1, the leftover is again counted as rollover. Repeating this across epochs can keep rollover rewards perpetually un-emitted, depriving stakers of rewards indefinitely.",
      "suggested_fix": "In the new-period branch, compute the rate from the full reserved amount:\n- Replace `rewardRate = rewardAmount / epochTimeRemaining;` with `rewardRate = totalRewardAmount / epochTimeRemaining;`\nAlso ensure pool sync uses consistent reserve/rate semantics (i.e., rewardReserve matches what rewardRate will emit over the period)."
